<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Transcribed by Divyash Maheshwari -->
<worksheet xml:id="se1f">
  <title>Linear Algebra Sample Exam 1F</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <em>Work done on scratch paper will not be graded. You don't need to show your work on pages 1 and 2.</em>
    </p>
  </introduction>
<!-- Page 1 -->
    <page>
        <exercise workspace="1in">
            <statement>
            <p>
                (5 points) Indicate whether the situations are <term>possible</term> or <term>impossible</term>.
            </p>
            <tabular>
                <col width="76%" />
                <col width="12%" />
                <col width="12%" />
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell>possible</cell>
                    <cell>impossible</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>a) <m>A</m> is a <m>3 \times 2</m> matrix with linearly independent columns, and <m>\vec{b} \in \mathbb{R}^3</m> is not in the span of the columns of <m>A</m>.</p></cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>b) A set of vectors <m>\{ \vec{v}_i \}</m> for <m>i = 1, 2, \ldots , n</m> are linearly dependent. The only way that <m>\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n} c_i \vec{v}_i = \vec{0}</m> is when <m>c_i = 0</m> for <m>i = 1, 2, 3, \ldots , n</m>.</p></cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>c) Let <m>A</m> be a <m>6 \times 10</m> matrix. The only solution to <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> is <m>\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>.</p></cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>d) <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is a one-to-one linear transform, <m>A</m> is <m>5 \times 3</m>, and <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> does not have a solution for some <m>\vec{b} \in \mathbb{R}^5</m>.</p></cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>e) <m>T(\vec{x})</m> is a matrix transform that maps vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m> to <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m>, <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>. Matrix <m>A</m> is <m>m \times n</m>, and <m>\vec{b}</m> is a vector in <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m>.</p></cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    (7 points) Indicate <term>true</term> if the statement is true, otherwise, indicate <term>false</term>.
                </p>
                <tabular>
                    <col width="88%" />
                    <col width="6%" />
                    <col width="6%" />
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell>true</cell>
                        <cell>false</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><p>a) The transform <m>T( x_1 , x_2 ) = ( x_1 + 1, x_2 )</m> is a linear transform.</p></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><p>b) If <m>A</m> is the standard matrix for a linear transform that is one-to-one and onto, then <m>A</m> must be square.</p></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><p>c) If <m>\vec{x_1}</m> and <m>\vec{x_2}</m> solve the inhomogeneous system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>, <m>\vec{x_1} - \vec{x_2}</m> solves the homogeneous system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>.</p></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><p>d) If a set of vectors <m>\{ \vec{v_1} , \vec{v_2} , \vec{v_3} \}</m> is linearly independent, then any subset such as <m>\{ \vec{v_1} , \vec{v_2}</m> must also be linearly independent.</p></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><p>e) If vectors <m>\vec{x}</m>, <m>\vec{y}</m>, and <m>\vec{z}</m> are linearly dependent vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>, then their span is a plane in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.</p></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><p>f) If a consistent system has at least one free variable, then it has infinitely many solutions.</p></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><p>g) A matrix with <m>3</m> pivotal rows also has <m>3</m> pivotal columns.</p></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </page>
<!-- Page 2 -->
    <page>
        <exercise workspace="0.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    (2 points) Suppose <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} -1 &amp; 2 \\ 2 &amp; -4 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>B</m> is the standard matrix for a transform that reflects vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> over the line <m>x_1 +x_2 = 0</m>, and <m>C</m> is the standard matrix for the transform <m>T_C : \vec{x} \mapsto C\vec{x}</m>, where <m>C = BA</m>. In the grids below, sketch a) the range of the transform <m>\vec{x} \mapsto A\vec{x}</m>, and b) the range of the transform <m>\vec{x} \mapsto C\vec{x}</m>. You do not need to show your work.
                </p>
                <sidebyside widths="4% 32% 4% 32%">  
          <p> (a) </p>
          <image width="100%">
            <prefigure label="se1f-3a" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
            <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
            <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
              <grid-axes arrows="1" xlabel="x_1" ylabel="x_2"/>    
            </coordinates>
            </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>

          <p> (b)</p>
          <image width="100%">
            <prefigure label="se1f-3b" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
            <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
            <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
              <grid-axes arrows="1" xlabel="x_1" ylabel="x_2"/>    
            </coordinates>
            </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
          </sidebyside>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    (7 points) Fill in the blanks. You do not need to justify your reasoning.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>A</m> is <m>2 \times 2</m> and <m>T_A (\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is a linear transform that first reflects points in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> through the line <m>x_1 + x_2 = 0</m>, and then projects them onto the <m>x_1</m>-axis, then <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} a_1 &amp; a_2 \\ a_3 &amp; a_4 \end{pmatrix}</m> where <m>a_1 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m>, <m>a_2 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m>, <m>a_3 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m> and <m>a_4 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> is a <m>3 \times 2</m> non-zero matrix that is in RREF, <m>\vec{x} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m> is a solution to <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>, and every entry of <m>A</m> is either <m>1</m> or <m>0</m>. How many different matrices can you construct that meet all of these criteria, if any? If there are no such matrices you can write NONE. <m>\fbox{\phantom{\rule{3em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Suppose <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} 3 &amp; 0 \\3 &amp; 1 \end{pmatrix}</m> and the image of <m>x = \begin{pmatrix}c_1 \\ c_2 \end{pmatrix}</m> under the transform <m>T(x) = Ax</m> is <m>b = \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}</m>, then <m>c_1 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m> and <m>c_2 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{0pt}}\rule{0pt}{1.4ex}}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} k &amp; k \\ 3 &amp; 3 \end{pmatrix}</m>, then <m>\vec{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m> is in the span of the columns of <m>A</m> when <m>k = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>. Also, <m>\vec{w} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ h \end{pmatrix}</m> is a solution to <m>A\vec{w} = \vec{0}</m> when <m>h = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{4em}{1.4ex}}}</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Suppose <m>\vec{v_1} = \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 2 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\vec{v_2} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m>, and <m>\vec{y} = \begin{pmatrix} y_1 \\ y_2 \\ y_3 \end{pmatrix}</m>. If <m>A</m> has <m>2</m> pivots, then the transform <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> satisfies <m>T(\vec{v_1}) = 3T(\vec{v_2}) \neq \vec{0}</m>, <m>y_3 = 8</m>, and <m>\vec{y}</m> satisfies <m>A\vec{y} = \vec{0}</m>, then <m>y_1 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>, <m>y_2 = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The linear system whose augmented matrix is <m>\left( \begin{array}{cc|c} 2 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 \\ 4 &amp; k &amp; 1 \end{array} \right)</m> is inconsistent when <m>k = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Consider the linear system <m>3x + hy = 7</m> and <m>9x + 15y = k</m>. The system will have no solutions when <m>k = 0</m> and <m>h = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>. But the system will have an infinite number of solutions when <m>h = 5</m> and <m>k = \fbox{\phantom{\rule{2em}{1.4ex}}}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

<!-- Page 3 -->
    <page>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    (2 points) Suppose <m>\vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}</m> are non-zero vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>, and that <m>\vec{v_1}</m> and <m>\vec{v_2}</m> span a plane. Then <m>S = \text{Span}\{\vec{v_1},\vec{v_2}\}</m> is equal to which of the sets below? You do not need to show your work.
                </p>
                <tabular>
                    <col width="45%" />
                    <col width="20%" />
                    <col width="20%" />
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell><p>equal to <m>S</m></p></cell>
                        <cell><p>not equal to <m>S</m></p></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><p>a) <m>W = \text{Span}\{ \vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}, \vec{0} \}</m></p></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><p>b) <m>U = \text{Span}\{\vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2}, \vec{v_1} + 12\vec{v_2}</m></p></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><p>c) <m>X = \text{Span}\{\vec{v_1} + \vec{v_2}, -\vec{v_1} - \vec{v_2}\}</m></p></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><p>d) <m>Y = \text{Span}\{\vec{v_1} + \vec{v_2}, \vec{v_2}\}</m></p></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    (2 points) Please show your work for this question.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Determine all values of <m>k</m>, if any, so that the span of the vectors is only a plane in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>. Please show your work.
                    <md>
                        v_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -k \end{pmatrix}, \quad v_2 =\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}, \quad v_3 = \begin{pmatrix} k \\ -1 \\ 5k \end{pmatrix}
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </page>
</worksheet>